{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-80643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =a{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+6xy+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =b{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{2}+2, \\\\ {f}_{y}(-1,1) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(a{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+6xy+2x) \\\\  & =2a{x}^{2}y+6x+0 \\\\  & =2a{x}^{2}y+6x \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(b{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =3b{x}^{2}y+6x+0 \\\\  & =3b{x}^{2}y+6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a{x}^{2}y+6x=3b{x}^{2}y+6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of ${x}^{2}y$, we see that $a=\\frac{3b}{2}.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{y}(-1,1)=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,1) & =3 \\\\ b(-1)^{3}(1)+3(-1)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ -b+3+2 & =3 \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na=\\frac{3b}{2}=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-80643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "a=1,\\,b=2"] ["math" "a=2,\\,b=1"] ["math" "a=2,\\,b=3"] ["math" "a=3,\\,b=2"] ["math" "a=3,\\,b=6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "a=3,\\,b=2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=axy-5{y}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}-bxy.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(axy-5{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =ax-10y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-bxy) \\\\  & =2x-by\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax-10y=2x-by.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ respectively, we see that $a=2$ and $b=10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-133221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "a=2,\\,b=10"] ["math" "a=2,\\,b=5"] ["math" "a=3,\\,b=7"] ["math" "a=5,\\,b=2"] ["math" "a=6,\\,b=9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "a=2,\\,b=10"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=a{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}+3,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{3}+bxy-8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(a{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}-8y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}+bxy-8) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+by\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}-8y=6{x}^{2}+by.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of ${x}^{2}$ and $y,$ respectively, we see that $a=6$ and $b=-8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-65136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$a=-2$, $b=4$"] ["text" "$a=-3$, $b=8$"] ["text" "$a=3$, $b=-4$"] ["text" "$a=6$, $b=-8$"] ["text" "$a=6$, $b=8$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$a=6$, $b=-8$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80766" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80766" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}=b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x}$ and $x{y}^{2},$ respectively, we see that $b=1$ and $a=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80766" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-80766" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80766" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$a=-1$, $b=2$"] ["text" "$a=-2$, $b=1$"] ["text" "$a=-e$, $b=2$"] ["text" "$a=1$, $b=2$"] ["text" "$a=2$, $b=e$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80766" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$a=-2$, $b=1$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =ay(1+x){e}^{x}+y{e}^{y}+4, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x(1+y){e}^{y}-bx{e}^{x}-1, \\\\ {f}_{x}(1,1) & =4+5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(ay(1+x){e}^{x}+y{e}^{y}+4) \\\\  & =a(1+x){e}^{x}+(1+y){e}^{y} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(1+y){e}^{y}-bx{e}^{x}-1) \\\\  & =(1+y){e}^{y}-b(1+x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1+x){e}^{x}+(1+y){e}^{y}=(1+y){e}^{y}-b(1+x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x},$ respectively, we see that $a=-b.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{x}(1,1)=4+5e$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =4+5e \\\\ a(1)(1+(1))e+(1)e+4 & =4+5e \\\\ 2ae+e+4 & =4+5e \\\\ (2a+1)e & =5e \\\\ 2a+1 & =5 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nb=-a=-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-80647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$a=1$, $b=-2$"] ["text" "$a=2$, $b=-2$"] ["text" "$a=2$, $b=-4$"] ["text" "$a=2$, $b=1$"] ["text" "$a=4$, $b=2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$a=2$, $b=-2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=(x-1){e}^{x}+2xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-y)=4xy-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2x)=4xy-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2})=x{e}^{x}+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1){e}^{x}+2xy)=x{e}^{x}+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-80753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-80753" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=8xy-5{x}^{3}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=6x{y}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{e}^{3x+2y}+3{y}^{2})=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{3x+2y}+6xy)=6{e}^{3x+2y}+6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{2}-15{x}^{2}y)=8y-15{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-5{x}^{3})=8y-15{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=18{x}^{2}y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{3})=6{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133250" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-133250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133250" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-36518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+4y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=0$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-36518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+4y)=4 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-36518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-36518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-36518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-36518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{3}y-x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{x+y}-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x)={x}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-y)={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}-2y)={e}^{x+y}-2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}-2x)={e}^{x+y}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x-1})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65127" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Devel