14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{y+1})=2x{e}^{y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7x-3y)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-7y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-133331" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133331" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133331" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxx}(1,1)$ if ${f}_{xy}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}(2+xy).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxx}(x,y) & ={f}_{xyx}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy}(2+xy)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}(2+xy)+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}(3+xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxx}(1,1)=(1)^{2}{e}^{1\\cdot 1}(3+(1)(1))=4e\\,\\,\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-65130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2e"] ["math" "2{e}^{2}"] ["math" "3e"] ["math" "4e"] ["math" "4{e}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4e"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first, second and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ What is the value of ${f}_{yx}(1,0)+{f}_{xyx}(1,0)-{f}_{xxy}(1,0)$ given that ${f}_{yx}(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "As the function and its first, second, and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ we must have ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)={f}_{xxy}(x,y).$ \n\nTherefore, we can swap the order of the subscripts in the first term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(1,0)+{f}_{xyx}(1,0)-{f}_{xxy}(1,0) & ={f}_{yx}(1,0)+{f}_{xyx}(1,0)-{f}_{xyx}(1,0) \\\\  & ={f}_{yx}(1,0)+\\cancel{{f}_{xxy}(1,0)}-\\cancel{{f}_{xxy}(1,0)} \\\\  & ={f}_{yx}(1,0) \\\\  & =2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0)-(1)^{2}(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot 0)+1 \\\\  & =2-0+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-65133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-65133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y},\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}=-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{yz}\\cdot (\\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{yz}}{(x+y)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot ({e}^{yz}+yz{e}^{yz}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{yz}(1+yz)}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}}{(1+1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =-\\frac{{e}^{1\\cdot 0}(1+1\\cdot 0)}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133382" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-133382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133382" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${f}_{yzx}(2,1,3)$ given that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{xy}(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yzx}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{xyz}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x-y+z}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-y+z}}-\\frac{1}{x+y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point  $(2,1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yzx}(2,1,3) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2-1+3}}-\\frac{1}{2+1+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-133395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{xyx}(1,\\pi )$ if ${f}_{xx}(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyx}(x,y) & ={f}_{xxy}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{xx}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{e}^{xy}\\cdot (-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{e}^{xy}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{xyx}(1,\\pi )={1}^{3}{e}^{1\\cdot \\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi ))=-{e}^{\\pi }\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a761aa078d91dfbb0fcfeb9e1752de9fcf3d7f60df63467ab2d98336b55ad3d"} {"question" "q-133371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{e}^{\\pi }"] ["math" "1-{e}^{\\pi }"] ["math" "{e}^{-\\pi }+1"] ["math" "{e}^{1-\\pi }"] ["math" "{e}^{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "q-133371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{e}^{\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/state.json" "file_sha256" "edb14c8ed6862d85f14a008f71339d54848dc0e64e3a73cc97fd27aefdbffd1d"} {"question" "e-3152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b,$ if \n\n$$\nf_x(x,y) = 6xy+axy^3-5, \\qquad\nf_y(x,y) = 3x^2+bx^2y^2, \\qquad\nf_y(2,-1) = -24.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/example-3152.json" "file_sha256" "a432651050526d82d2fb8f7e8b43e81e8102b1fa01032c81fa32751769657df1"} {"question" "e-3152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we compute the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+ax{y}^{3}-5) \\\\  & =6x+3ax{y}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =6x+3ax{y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+b{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6x+2bx{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. So, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yx}(x,y) \\\\ 6x+3ax{y}^{2} & =6x+2bx{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $xy^2,$ we see that $3a=2b.$ \n\nNow, since $f_y(2,-1)=-24,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,-1) & =-24 \\\\ 3(2)^{2}+b(2)^{2}(-1)^{2} & =-24 \\\\ 12+4b & =-24 \\\\ 4b & =-36 \\\\ b & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & =2b \\\\ 3a & =2(-9) \\\\ 3a & =-18 \\\\ a & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-6$ and $b=-9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/example-3152.json" "file_sha256" "a432651050526d82d2fb8f7e8b43e81e8102b1fa01032c81fa32751769657df1"} {"question" "e-3154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=x+2y$ and $f_y(x,y)=2y-x$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$ and $f_y(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$\n\n- $f_x(x,y)=-\\sin{x}\\sin{y}$ and $f_y(x,y)=\\cos{x}\\cos{y}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/example-3154.json" "file_sha256" "f63a9b5dcb3a57b7384cb6ba682a2cedf55d9c9503f58266ff04c6f3a98d8acf"} {"question" "e-3154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is,\n\n$$\nf_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y).\n$$\n\nLet's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+2y)=2, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) \\neq f_{yx}(x,y),$ the first pair could *not* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5(x+y)^{4})=20(x+y)^{3}, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5(x+y)^{4})=20(x+y)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) = f_{yx}(x,y),$ the second pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y),$  the third pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/example-3154.json" "file_sha256" "f63a9b5dcb3a57b7384cb6ba682a2cedf55d9c9503f58266ff04c6f3a98d8acf"} {"question" "e-3153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y,z).$ Given that all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial z\\partial x} = -e^xy\\sin{(yz)},\n$$\n\n \n\nevaluate the following expression at the point $(1,\\pi,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial z\\partial x\\partial z}+ \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f }{\\partial x \\partial z \\partial y}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/example-3153.json" "file_sha256" "1ae070b8dbf59a5e8066a129dfaa851911abe84280edc8192444c240c7fef806"} {"question" "e-3153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂z∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-{e}^{x}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)-{e}^{x}yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)+yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,\\pi,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =-{e}^{(1)}(\\pi )^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)=e{\\pi }^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =-{e}^{(1)}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)+(\\pi )(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1))=e\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y}{|}_{(1,\\pi ,1)} & =e{\\pi }^{2}+e\\pi =e\\pi (\\pi +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044468/example-3153.json" "file_sha256" "1ae070b8dbf59a5e8066a129dfaa851911abe84280edc8192444c240c7fef806"}] "usage" {"e-3154" [] "q-36518" [] "q-65127" [] "q-65136" [] "e-3152" [] "e-3153" []} "basis" 1652 "retractions" [[17592186125103 :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if\n\n$f_{x}(x, y) = ax^{2}y - 4y^{2} + 3, f_{y}(x, y) = 2x^{3} + bxy - 8$."] [17592186124914 :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x, y)$?\n\n1. $f_{x}(x, y) = x + 4y$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = 3x - y$\n2. $f_{x}(x, y) = 2x$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = 0$\n3. $f_{x}(x, y) = 2x + \\cos x\\cos y$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = e^{y} - \\sin x\\sin y$"] [17592186125102 :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x, y)$?\n\n1. $f_{x}(x, y) = x^{3}y - x$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = xy^{3} - y$\n2. $f_{x}(x, y) = e^{x + y} - 2y$ and $f_{y}(x, y) = e^{x + y} - 2x$\n3. $f_{x}(x, y) = \\sqrt{x - 1}$ and $f_{y}(x, y) =-3$"] [17592186124167 :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if\n$f_x(x,y) = 6xy+axy^3-5, \\qquad f_y(x,y) = 3x^2+bx^2y^2, \\qquad f_y(2,-1) = -24$."] [17592186124167 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{xy}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(f_{x}(x, y)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy + axy^{3} - 5) \\\\\n&= 6x + 3axy^{2} - 0 \\\\\n&= 6x + 3axy^{2} \\\\\nf_{yx}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(f_{y}(x, y)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} + bx^{2}y^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6x + 2bxy^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. So, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{xy}(x, y) &= f_{yx}(x, y) \\\\\n6x + 3axy^{2} &= 6x + 2bxy^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $xy^2$, we see that $3a=2b$.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf_y(2,-1)=-24\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{y}(2,-1) &= -24 \\\\\n3(2)^{2} + b(2)^{2}(-1)^{2} &= -24 \\\\\n12 + 4b &= -24 \\\\\n4b &= -36 \\\\\nb &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a &= 2b \\\\\n3a &= 2(-9) \\\\\n3a &= -18 \\\\\na &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-6$ and $b=-9$."] [17592186124169 :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$?\n\n1. $f_x(x,y)=x+2y$ and $f_y(x,y)=2y-x$\n2. $f_x(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$ and $f_y(x,y)=5(x+y)^4$\n3. $f_x(x,y)=-\\sin{x}\\sin{y}$ and $f_y(x,y)=\\cos{x}\\cos{y}$"] [17592186124169 :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is,\n\n$$\nf_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y)\n$$\n\nLet's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n$f_{xy}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + 2y) = 2,; f_{yx}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y - x) =-1$.\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) \\neq f_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could *not* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$.\n- For the second pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n$f_{xy}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5(x + y)^{4}) = 20(x + y)^{3},; f_{yx}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(5(x + y)^{4}) = 20(x + y)^{3}$.\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y) = f_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$.\n- For the third pair, the mixed partial derivatives are\n$f_{xy}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sin x\\sin y) =-\\sin x\\cos y,; f_{yx}(x, y)|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\cos x\\cos y) =-\\sin x\\cos y$.\nSince $f_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y)$, the third pair *could* represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186124168 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y,z)$. Given that all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, and that\n\n$\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial z\\partial x} = -e^xy\\sin{(yz)}$,\n\nevaluate the following expression at the point $(1,\\pi,1)$:\n\n$\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial z\\partial x\\partial z}+ \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial x \\partial z \\partial y}$"] [17592186124168 :question/worked-solution "Since the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, the mixed partial derivatives must be equal.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} &= \\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂z∂x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂z}(-e^{x}y\\sin (yz)) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}y^{2}\\cos (yz)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂z∂x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-e^{x}y\\sin (yz)) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\sin (yz) - e^{x}yz\\cos (yz) \\\\\n&=-e^{x}(\\sin (yz) + yz\\cos (yz))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,\\pi,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & _{(1, π, 1)} &= -e^{(1)}(π)^{2}\\cos (π \\cdot 1) = eπ^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} & _{(1, π, 1)}|=-e^{(1)}(\\sin (π \\cdot 1) + (π)(1)\\cos (π \\cdot 1)) = eπ\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} \\mid _{(1, π, 1)} + \\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂x∂z∂y} \\mid _{(1, π, 1)} &= eπ^{2} + eπ = eπ(π + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122507 17592186122508 17592186122509 17592186122510 17592186122511 17592186123635 17592186123636 17592186123637 17592186123638 17592186123639 17592186123641 17592186123642 17592186123643 17592186123644 17592186123645] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122506 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122506 :answer-field/id] [17592186122506 :answer-field/key] [17592186122506 :answer-field/type] [17592186123634 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123634 :answer-field/id] [17592186123634 :answer-field/key] [17592186123634 :answer-field/type] [17592186123640 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123640 :answer-field/id] [17592186123640 :answer-field/key] [17592186123640 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
